# BAGWORK environment template
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real values:  cp .env.example .env.local
# `.env.local` is gitignored; this template is committed so every machine knows which
# keys are required.

# ---- Database (Supabase Postgres) ----
# Copy both strings from Supabase Dashboard -> Connect:
#   Transaction pooler (6543) -> DATABASE_URL : runtime, avoids connection exhaustion
#   Direct connection (5432)  -> DIRECT_URL   : schema changes and migrations
#
# REGION — this must be a US project, and it must move together with the Vercel functions.
#
# The functions run in `iad1` (US East, Washington D.C. — see `vercel.json`), so the database
# has to be in `us-east-1` to sit beside them. Every query is paid at wire speed, and a US app
# talking to a Tokyo database spends roughly 150ms per statement doing nothing at all; against
# this app's remote pooler — where a single dashboard request is several statements — that is
# the whole difference between a console that feels instant and one that feels broken.
#
# Moving only one half does not help. A US function calling a Japanese pooler is the same
# latency as before, just measured from the other end, which is why `vercel.json` and these two
# URLs are a pair rather than two independent settings.
#
# The region is written into the hostname, so it is checkable at a glance:
#   us-east-1        -> aws-0-us-east-1.pooler.supabase.com      (correct)
#   ap-northeast-1   -> aws-0-ap-northeast-1.pooler.supabase.com (Tokyo — wrong, replace it)
# `<region>` below is that hostname segment. A project created after the region move may use an
# `aws-1-` prefix instead of `aws-0-`; copy whatever the dashboard actually shows rather than
# editing the prefix by hand.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# ---- Supabase API keys ----
# Not required. The app reaches Postgres directly through Prisma (`DATABASE_URL` /
# `DIRECT_URL`), and `src/lib/supabase/*` was removed as unused in the technical-debt pass.
# You only need these if you later add a supabase-js (PostgREST / storage / realtime) path
# — `prisma/rls.sql` already grants the `anon` role read access to live campaigns, and
# Row Level Security denies every other table, so that path would be safe to switch on.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="sb_publishable_..."
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY="sb_secret_..."

# ---- Privy (auth + wallets) ----
# Dashboard -> App settings
NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_APP_ID=""
PRIVY_APP_SECRET=""
# ---- Privy login methods ----
# Wallet-ONLY, and it is not configurable here any more.
#
# The modal offers exactly one thing: a Solana wallet. That is enforced in code
# (`loginMethods: ["wallet"]` in `components/auth/auth-provider.tsx`) because the dashboard has
# email, SMS and embedded wallets disabled — the two must agree, or the modal would offer a method
# the app then refuses.
#
# There used to be a NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_WALLET_ONLY flag here that kept email as a secondary option.
# It is gone; nothing reads it.

# ---- Privy: linking social accounts (SAME app) ----
# Proving an X / TikTok / Instagram handle runs on this one Privy app, on the account that is already
# signed in: the browser calls Privy's link flow (`useLinkAccount` through `useAuth().linkSocial`),
# Privy adds the account to the user, and `POST /api/profile/socials` re-reads the handle from that
# user before storing it on `User.socials` — the field the submission gate matches post links against.
#
# No second app, and no extra variables. There used to be one, and it was removed: two Privy providers
# on one origin share a session, so a second app could sign people out of bagwork.
#
# Operator setup, in this app's dashboard: enable X/Twitter, TikTok and Instagram for *linking* (they
# stay disabled as login methods — signing in is still a wallet), and allow this deployment's origin.
# Signing in, launching and browsing all work with no social account linked at all.

# ---- Privy webhooks (server safety net) ----
# The signing secret for POST /api/webhooks/privy, from the endpoint's detail page in the Privy
# dashboard (a `whsec_…` value). Server-only — never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# The route creates/updates the user row and fills in the default payout wallet when a user is
# created, authenticates, or links a wallet — so an account is complete even when the browser never
# reaches /api/auth/session. Deliveries are verified with the Privy SDK's Svix verifier and refused
# with 503 while this is unset, so an unsigned payload can never write a wallet address.
#
# Dashboard setup: add an endpoint at
#   https://bag-work.fun/api/webhooks/privy
# and enable the events user.created, user.authenticated, user.linked_account, user.wallet_created.
PRIVY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# ---- Site (canonical domain) ----
# The domain the app publishes in canonical tags, Open Graph URLs, the sitemap and robots.txt.
# Read by src/lib/site.ts. Leave it unset on Vercel: `VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL` is used as the
# fallback, so the project's production domain is picked up automatically (currently
# https://bag-work.fun) with nothing to configure and nothing to update if the domain changes.
# Set it by hand only to point the metadata somewhere else — a staging domain, for instance.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=""

# ---- Solana ----
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOLANA_CLUSTER="devnet"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOLANA_RPC_URL="https://api.devnet.solana.com"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOLANA_WS_URL="wss://api.devnet.solana.com"

# ---- Helius (server-side Solana RPC + token/asset data) ----
# Read by src/lib/helius.ts and used as the server RPC endpoint. Server-only: never
# prefix this with NEXT_PUBLIC_, or the key ships to the browser.
HELIUS_API_KEY=""

# ---- Admin console (/adminportal) ----
# Password for the internal moderation console. With no value set the console is closed
# to everyone rather than open, so set a real one before using it.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=""

# ---- Bagwork treasuries (Solana) ----
# TWO wallets, one per funding model. Never expose either key to the client (no NEXT_PUBLIC_).
#
# FIXED treasury — holds publisher budgets. Fixed-budget Bag funding transfers land here and
# fixed-bag payouts are signed from here. Accepts a JSON byte array or a base58 string.
TREASURY_FIXED_PRIVATE_KEY=""
# Optional, and only for the rare case where the address is known but the key is held elsewhere
# (hardware wallet, multisig). When set WITHOUT a key, funding still works but automatic payouts
# are refused with a clear reason.
TREASURY_FIXED_ADDRESS=""
#
# FEE treasury — holds redirected trading fees. Fee detection (cron + webhook) watches this
# address only, fee→USDC swaps sign with this key, and flywheel payouts leave from here.
# Deliberately NO fallback to the legacy key: a flywheel that drew from publisher budgets would
# be exactly the mixing the dual-treasury split exists to prevent. Unset = flywheel creation is
# refused (503) with a clear message.
TREASURY_FEE_PRIVATE_KEY=""
TREASURY_FEE_ADDRESS=""
#
# Legacy: the original single treasury. It maps to the FIXED treasury, so an existing deployment
# keeps working with no change — do not set it once TREASURY_FIXED_PRIVATE_KEY exists.
# TREASURY_PRIVATE_KEY=""
# Legacy address-only fallback for the FIXED treasury:
# TREASURY_WALLET_ADDRESS=""
#
# ---- How these wallets are funded (read before worrying about the balance) ----
#
# Neither wallet has a budget configured anywhere in code. A fresh deployment is seeded with a few
# dollars of SOL (and for FEE, a little of the coin being redirected) purely so the first fee sync
# and the first swap can pay their own network fees. That is bootstrap money, NOT a ceiling: nothing
# reserves it and no code path refuses to spend past it.
#
# Real spend scales with what arrives:
#   FIXED - publisher funding transfers, as budgets are created and topped up.
#   FEE   - redirected creator fees, deposit by deposit, plus whatever an operator sends to the FEE
#           address (top it up whenever you like - it is an ordinary wallet).
#
# Outflows are judged on live balances: is there SOL for rent/gas, is there enough of the payout
# mint? The only *limit* on a payout is the paying bag's own budget - a flywheel bag can never spend
# another bag's fees (src/lib/payouts/budget.ts, and see the R1 guard in src/lib/payouts/settle.ts).
# There is deliberately no platform-wide dollar cap and no "keep $X in the treasury" reserve.

# ---- Scheduled jobs ----
# The fee sync (fees → USDC → budget) runs daily at 00:00 via Vercel Cron; the payout sweep
# (automatic payouts that could not be sent at approval time) runs daily at 01:00. Both accept
# either of these secrets as `Authorization: Bearer <secret>` (the fee sync also allows a signed-in
# session; the payout sweep additionally allows the admin cookie, because money moves there).
CRON_SECRET=""
FEE_SYNC_SECRET=""
PAYOUT_RUN_SECRET=""

# ---- Alchemy (backup RPC + treasury webhooks) ----
# Alchemy is the *secondary* provider: Helius stays primary. Reads that matter (transaction
# lookups, balances, signature statuses, funding and payout verification) retry against Alchemy,
# then against the public cluster endpoint, and log which provider answered.
#
# Either set ALCHEMY_RPC_URL to the full URL from the Alchemy dashboard, or set just the key and
# the app builds the documented Solana host for the active cluster. Leave both empty to run on
# Helius alone — the chain simply skips Alchemy.
ALCHEMY_RPC_URL=""
# Optional: used only to construct the RPC URL when ALCHEMY_RPC_URL is not set.
ALCHEMY_API_KEY=""

# The *per-webhook signing key* from the webhook's detail page in the Alchemy dashboard — not the
# app's auth token. POST /api/webhooks/alchemy verifies every delivery as HMAC-SHA256 over the raw
# body (`X-Alchemy-Signature`), and refuses everything while this is unset.
ALCHEMY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# ---- Venice AI (submission review) ----
# Server-only. Used by src/lib/ai/venice.ts to score a submission against its campaign brief.
# Never prefix this with NEXT_PUBLIC_: it is a billable key and must not reach the browser.
# Get one at https://venice.ai -> API. Leave empty and every submission simply stays in
# "pending review" for a human, which is the safe failure — never an invented verdict.
VENICE_API_KEY=""

# Optional. Pins the model instead of discovering one. When empty the cheapest `grok-*` in Venice's
# catalogue is used, falling back to the cheapest model it lists. Set it to control cost explicitly.
VENICE_MODEL=""

# ---- BAG token (Solana) ----
# Mint address of the BAG token, used for the 2.5% buyback-and-burn.
# Leave empty until the mint is deployed - burn flows stay disabled while unset, and
# /dashboard/token (Token Analytics) runs in "Launching Soon" mode rather than showing
# placeholder numbers. Setting this is the entire launch procedure for that page: the
# contract address, its copy button and both explorer links switch on from it, and the
# market/buyback providers in src/lib/token/analytics.ts start being asked for data.
# A test or stand-in mint must not be put here: known stand-ins are refused in code (see
# STAND_IN_MINTS in src/lib/solana/burn.ts) and resolve to "pre-launch", so pointing this at a
# throwaway token cannot make the page report one. Nothing else configures the token.
BAG_TOKEN_MINT=
# ---- Launch-on-Bagwork (pump.fun) ----
# Arms the Launch Wizard: the "Launch a flywheel coin" flow that creates a coin on pump.fun and
# locks 100% of its creator fees to the FEE treasury. The launcher signs two transactions — three
# when a dev buy is chosen — because the whole launch does not fit in one Solana packet (1232 bytes).
# Both are verified on-chain before any bag is created.
#
# Fails closed. Accepted as on: true, TRUE, "true", 1, yes, on (trimmed, case-insensitive, quotes
# stripped). Anything else leaves the flow switched off and the build route answers 503 with the
# reason. This has to be a deliberate switch because nothing in CI can exercise a launch.
#
# There is no devnet rehearsal to wait for: pump.fun's create and fee-share instructions are
# mainnet-only. The first real launch IS the test, which is why the flow builds, simulates and
# verifies on-chain before it creates anything.
#
# Vercel note: an environment change does not reach a deployment that is already running. Add or
# change this, then redeploy — otherwise the running build keeps reporting the old value.
#
# When it IS on, launching also requires the FEE treasury above (TREASURY_FEE_PRIVATE_KEY or
# TREASURY_FEE_ADDRESS): an armed flag with no treasury is still refused, because a launch with
# nowhere to lock fees to would create a coin whose creator fees go to a wallet nobody controls.
#
# Money rule: the launcher's own connected wallet pays the create, the rent and any dev buy. The
# FEE treasury is never a payer here — it only ever receives the coin's creator fees, and later
# swaps them to USDC to fund and pay that coin's bag.
PUMP_LAUNCH_ENABLED=""
# ---- Fee redirect swaps ----
# The smallest FEE → USDC swap worth sending, in USD. Default 20.
#
# A swap is not free (signature + priority fees), and early creator fees on a new coin are tiny, so
# converting them one deposit at a time burns more SOL than it converts. Below this floor the
# deposit is recorded, left in the fee wallet and re-quoted on a later sync — no transaction is
# built, signed or sent. Set to 0 to swap whatever arrives.
FEE_SWAP_MIN_USD="20"

# This is a cost-effectiveness floor, NOT a budget. It says "converting this deposit would cost more
# than it yields"; it does not cap what a wallet may spend, and it has nothing to do with how the
# treasury above was seeded.
#
# Optional: the most a single fee swap will bid in priority fees, in lamports. Default 2000000
# (0.002 SOL). Raise it if swaps stop landing on a congested chain. This is a ceiling on a
# transaction fee, never a limit on how much a bag can spend.
FEE_SWAP_PRIORITY_LAMPORTS=""
